facts:
Between two whole numbers, $10$ equal intervals each show **one tenth** ($0.1$).
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(6.6,0);
\foreach \k in {0,1,...,10} {\draw (0.6*\k,0.14)--(0.6*\k,-0.14);}
\node[below] at (0,-0.14) {$0$};
\node[below] at (6,-0.14) {$1$};
\draw[|<->|] (0,0.75)--(0.6,0.75);
\node[above] at (0.3,0.8) {$0.1$};
\node[above] at (3,1.5) {$10$ equal intervals};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Zooming into one tenth, $10$ smaller intervals each show **one hundredth** ($0.01$).
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(6.6,0);
\foreach \k in {0,1,...,10} {\draw (0.6*\k,0.14)--(0.6*\k,-0.14);}
\node[below] at (0,-0.14) {$0.3$};
\node[below] at (6,-0.14) {$0.4$};
\draw[|<->|] (0,0.75)--(0.6,0.75);
\node[above] at (0.3,0.8) {$0.01$};
\node[above] at (3,1.5) {one tenth, zoomed in};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

